-3223671 |access-date=7 November 2026 |website=DNA India |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261107054138/https://www.dnaindia.com/bollywood/report-ramayana-box-office-collection-day-1-despite-positive-reviews-ranbir-yash-film-trails-adipurush-dhurandhar-2-jawan-but-beats-animal-pathaan-3223671 |archive-date=7 November 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Mateen |first=Zoya |date=5 November 2026|title=India's costliest film retells a beloved Hindu epic - but divides critics |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cm93zv62v1n7o |website=bbc.com |location=Delhi, India |publisher=BBC News |access-date=5 November 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261105110701/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cm93zv62v1n7o |archive-date=5 November 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>}} It is the [[List of Hindi films of 2026#Box office collection|second highest-grossing Hindi film of 2026]],  [[List of Indian films of 2026#Box office collection|third highest-grossing Indian film of 2026]], and [[List of highest domestic net collection of Hindi films#Original films|sixteenth highest-grossing Hindi film domestically]].

== Plot ==
The power-seeking ''[[Asura]]s'' have long been in conflict with the ''[[Deva (Hinduism)|Devas]]''. Led by [[Indra]] and blessed by Lord [[Vishnu]],  the ''Devas'' defeat the ''Asuras'', led by [[List of characters in the Ramayana|Sumali]]. As the ''Devas'' rule over Lanka, Sumali's daughter [[Kaikashi]] urges her son [[Ravana#Etymology|Dashanan]] to avenge their defeat.

Dashanan undertakes years of penance and meditation until [[Brahma]] intervenes. When Brahma rejects his request for [[immortality]], Dashanan instead asks for invincibility against all supernatural beings, which Brahma grants. Returning to Lanka, Dashanan single-handedly defeats the ''Devas'' and claims the throne as King Ravana. He then launches a bloody campaign to conquer the realms of the universe. After learning that Ravana had not sought invincibility against humans, Indra appeals to [[Vishnu]] for help. Vishnu chooses [[Rama|Prince Rama]] of [[Ayodhya (Ramayana)|Ayodhya]] as his seventh ''[[avatar]]''.

In Ayodhya, Rama is born to [[Kausalya|Kaushalya]], one of King [[Dasharatha]]'s three wives. He grows to be a virtuous bachelor and is well-trained in the martial arts, eventually becoming the most favored among Dasharatha's four sons. Vishvamitra seeks Dasharatha's help in defeating Tataka and Subahu, two ''[[rakshasa]]s'' (demons) who disrupt sacrificial rites. Rama volunteers and is accompanied by his loyal half-brother [[Lakshmana]], with whom he defeats the ''rakshasas''. At Vishvamitra's advice, Rama travels to [[Mithila (region)|Mithila]], where he successfully lifts the [[Pinaka (Hinduism)|''Pinaka'']] ([[Shiva]]'s bow) during a royal [[Svayamvara|''swayamvara'']], although he breaks it while attempting to string it. This feat secures him the hand of Princess [[Sita]] in marriage. On their journey, Rama encounters Vishnu's sixth ''avatar'' [[Parashurama]], who challenges him to a duel for breaking the ''Pinaka''. Recognizing Rama's divine prowess, Parashurama relinquishes the [[Sharanga (Hindu mythology)|''Sharanga'']] (Vishnu's bow) to him and remarks that his era ends and Rama's begins.

The elderly Dasharatha tells Rama of his desire to crown Rama as his successor. [[Manthara]], the maidservant of [[Kaikeyi]] (one of Dasharatha's wives), persuades her mistress to invoke a promise the king made upon their marriage: that her son would inherit the throne. Kaikeyi demands that her son, [[Bharata (Ramayana)|Bharata]], succeed Dasharatha and Rama to be exiled for fourteen years. Bound by filial duty and ''[[dharma]]'', Rama renounces his claim to the throne and departs for the wilderness along with Sita and Lakshmana. Dasharatha subsequently dies of grief. Bharata visits Rama in the forest and implores him to return to Ayodhya and assume the throne, but Rama refuses. Bharata reluctantly agrees to govern the kingdom in his brother's stead. Meanwhile, Ravana kills his brother-in-law in a duel and subsequently commands his widowed sister [[Shurpanakha]] to find another husband.

After encountering ''rakshasas'' along their journey, Rama, Sita, and Lakshmana settle in the forest of [[Panchavati]] (under Ravana’s territory). They are visited by Shurpanakha, who becomes enamoured with Rama. After disguising as Sita and failing to seduce him, she attempts to attack Sita, but Lakshmana intervenes and slashes her face. In retaliation, her brother, Khara with an army of 14000 ''rakshasas'' attacks the trio, but Rama defeats them using his divine powers. When news of the incident reaches Ravana, he vows to avenge Shurpanakha's humiliation and destroy Rama, despite warnings that Rama is Vishnu's human ''avatar''.

The ''rakshasa'' [[Maricha]] approaches the trio's camp disguised as a beautiful deer, captivating Sita, who asks Rama to capture it. Suspecting that the deer is a ''rakshasa'' diversion, Rama leaves Sita under Lakshmana's protection and pursues Maricha. Upon being caught, Maricha mimics Rama's voice and cries out for Lakshmana's help. Alarmed, Sita prepares to leave the camp, but Lakshmana assures her that Rama is capable of defending himself and offers to go to his aid. Before departing, he draws a magical boundary around the camp to keep ''rakshasas'' out.

With both Rama and Lakshmana away, Ravana approaches the camp disguised as an [[Guru|ascetic]] seeking alms. Sita tests his knowledge by asking him to recite a line from the ''[[Rigveda]]''. As a great scholar of the Vedas, Ravana recites it correctly and eventually lures Sita out of Lakshmana's protective boundary. He then abducts Sita and takes her toward Lanka. Along the way, the vulture demigod [[Jatayu]] attempts to rescue her but is fatally wounded by Ravana. Rama and Lakshmana later find the dying Jatayu, who reveals Sita's abduction and warns them of Ravana's immense power and his followers. After burning Jatayu in a funeral pyre, a grief-stricken but resolute Rama vows to rescue Sita and destroy Ravana once and for all. 

In the [[Post-credits scene|mid-credits scene]], [[Hanuman]] emerges and chants Rama's name.

